<p>President Trump is scheduled to travel to Beijing this week for talks with Chinese President Xi Jinping, accompanied by a senior U.S. business delegation that includes Tesla and SpaceX CEO Elon Musk, Apple CEO Tim Cook and Boeing CEO Kelly Ortberg. The visit is scheduled for May 13-15, and the BBC reported that 17 U.S. executives are expected to join him. The delegation spans technology, aviation, finance, payments, agriculture and health-related companies, and sources described the agenda as focused on trade, artificial intelligence, rare earth minerals, agriculture, commercial aviation and other economic issues. Reports also described the trip as an effort to generate commercial agreements and purchase commitments during the Xi meeting. Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang was not on the delegation list, according to officials cited by EFE-based and Taiwanese reports, even though the trip includes executives from chip-related companies such as Qualcomm and Micron.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/qupSmPv71PisXR7ibgPA2UDVqd_lzLNMCnul1Lf1H4cPkIuO2Vk2vV507ILtbXSFXoyxPnwHZfk95mL8ALBQyl5y6RFxZDkcxuLg-2wNlJXmYG8ofSIRhBmwiGBhva3GPRyWbBIFN1MssKZoc45Zz1uvvoPbmCFObGp_HJQ08NNCuqO63nM1pXUIn1_7OtHQTmsq6n5GbV1WNQvR5F2k99gCT93jJkl32yZr0Y5cVSP1DkNy7WDn12EwNbk0n1B6spZxbbKiZpCppcW4FOVXZnfPfi5VAiM' alt='Elon Musk and Tim Cook head the list of executives invited to accompany President Trump to China for talks with Xi Jinping.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Headcount varies: EFE-linked and Apple-focused reports described a 16-person business delegation, while the BBC and Daily Pakistan reported 17 U.S. executives.</li><li>Trade bodies: Officials cited by noticiassin.com said Trump wants to discuss creating an investment board and a trade board with China.</li><li>Wider agenda: Africanews reported that trade, Iran and regional security were expected to be among the issues on the trip.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>White House official: A White House official’s list, publishe